<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · mountain</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mount Erciyes</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Mount Argaeus, Erciyes Dağı</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mount Erciyes, the ancient Mount Argaeus, is a dormant volcano south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kayseri">Kayseri</a> in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>, and at 3,917 metres the highest mountain in the region. The kings of Cappadocia ruled from Mazaca at its foot, and the mountain appeared on the city's coins. Its eruptions helped form the volcanic landscape of Cappadocia.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mount-erciyes.jpg" alt="Mount Erciyes" width="320" height="148" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Carole Raddato from FRANKFURT, Germany · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mount_Erciyes_(Argaeus),_Turkey_(31870998063)_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
